                    I am looking for a commercially minded Business Development & Partnerships VA to join my small, founder-led global business.



This is not a general admin VA role.



I am not looking for someone whose main strengths are inbox management, Canva graphics, diary organisation or data entry.



I am looking for someone who genuinely enjoys finding the right people, the right rooms and the right opportunities, and who gets satisfaction from seeing that research eventually turn into revenue.



You will work closely with a clear internal Revenue Pipeline system that gives you specific criteria, priorities and weekly direction. Your job is to turn that intelligence into excellent execution.



The role will initially be approximately 8–10 hours per month, with the possibility of increasing as the business grows.



Compensation will be:



US$8–$10/hour depending on experience

plus

5% commission on qualifying revenue directly attributable to opportunities or prospects you sourced



At current offer values, one successful conversion can result in a commission of several hundred dollars or more.



This role is therefore particularly suited to someone who enjoys performance-linked compensation and has previously succeeded in a commission-driven environment.



What you will actually be doing



Your work will primarily fall into four areas.



1. High-quality LinkedIn prospect research



You will research accomplished women globally who match very specific psychographic and commercial criteria.

We are not interested in buying generic lead lists or finding women simply because they have a certain job title, income level or follower count.

You need to be able to look at a person’s LinkedIn profile, company, body of work, recent activity and professional trajectory and determine:

why she may be commercially relevant;

what evidence suggests she is at an interesting transition or inflection point;

whether she appears to have the agency, sophistication and capacity appropriate for our work;

whether there is a natural reason for connection;

what context the founder should know before engaging personally.



You will be expected to distinguish between 20 vaguely suitable people and 3 genuinely exceptional prospects.

Quality matters far more than volume.



2. Podcast, speaking, community and partnership research



You will identify highly aligned:



podcasts;

interview shows;

curated women’s communities;

private founder or leadership communities;

workshops;

summits;

expert panels;

partnerships;

speaking opportunities;

other trusted environments where our ideal clients already gather.



Again, this is not about collecting lists.

A podcast with 500 deeply aligned listeners and an excellent host may be more valuable to us than a generic show with 50,000 downloads.



You must be able to investigate an opportunity and answer questions such as:

Who is the audience?

Who hosts it?

Why would this audience care about Elle’s work?

Does the host have trust with that audience?

Is the format conversational and interactive?

Are guest applications currently open?

What is the submission process?

What topics have already been covered?

Is this genuinely worth the founder’s time?



3. Outreach and opportunity execution



Once an opportunity has been approved, you may help with:

researching the correct contact;

completing guest applications;

submitting approved pitches;

gathering required bios, links and media assets;

following up appropriately;

managing logistical communication;

tracking responses;

making sure worthwhile opportunities do not disappear through lack of follow-through.



You may also support agreed LinkedIn business-development activity.



However:

You will never be expected to impersonate the founder.



Personal relationship conversations, referral requests, high-trust messages, fit conversations and sales conversations remain with Elle.



Your role is to create access, context and momentum.



4. Commercial follow-through



You will help ensure that opportunities already in motion do not quietly disappear.



That might include:

checking whether a promised reschedule has happened;

flagging a relationship that requires founder attention;

confirming upcoming commercial conversations;

making sure a guest application received a response;

noticing when a prospect or opportunity has gone stale;

bringing the right thing back to attention at the right time.



You are not expected to create reports for the sake of reporting.

I want commercial movement, not spreadsheet theatre.



You should NOT apply unless you can demonstrate experience in several of the following



I am specifically looking for someone who has previously done work involving:



LinkedIn prospect research;

lead qualification;

business development;

partnership research;

podcast guest sourcing;

speaking opportunity research;

founder/executive prospecting;

outreach;

appointment setting;

lead generation that required judgement rather than mass scraping;

researching communities, events or strategic partnerships;

commission-based sales or business development;

performance-based compensation.



You must be comfortable researching independently rather than waiting for step-by-step instructions for every action.



You also need very strong written English.



Commission experience is important



I am deliberately looking for someone who has previously worked in a role where part of their compensation depended on performance.

If you have never worked in a commission, bonus or results-driven commercial role, this probably isn't the right position for you.



For shortlisted candidates, I will ask for evidence of commission or performance-related earnings for three consecutive months from a previous role.

This can be anonymised/redacted.

I do not need to see confidential employer information, unrelated salary information, bank account numbers or personal identification.



I simply need credible evidence showing:

three consecutive months;

commission/performance payment received;

the amount earned.



This is a later-stage requirement. Do not send private financial documents with your initial application.



I am asking for this because I want someone who has demonstrated that she can actually perform in an environment where results matter, not simply someone who says she is “motivated by commission.”



This role will suit you if…



You enjoy the hunt.

You love finding the person nobody else noticed.

You can disappear into LinkedIn research and emerge with three extraordinary opportunities rather than a spreadsheet containing 300 irrelevant names.

You are commercially curious.

You ask yourself:  Why this person? Why now? Why us?

You notice patterns.

You follow through.

You are comfortable being measured on results.

You don't need constant supervision.

You care about the quality of a relationship, not just hitting an outreach quota.

You understand that business development can be sophisticated and human without becoming pushy or spammy.

And when something you discovered eventually becomes a client, you genuinely enjoy knowing:  I helped create that.



This role will NOT suit you if…



Please do not apply if:



you primarily want general administrative work;

your prospecting method is scraping hundreds of names;

you believe more outreach automatically means better outreach;

you send identical cold messages to everyone;

you have never researched prospects beyond job title and company;

you need detailed instructions for every small task;

you routinely submit generic applications without reading the job description;

you dislike commercial targets or performance-linked compensation;

you are uncomfortable with ambiguity and independent research;

you want a full-time role immediately;

you expect this small initial monthly engagement to function as full-time employment.



This role starts small intentionally.

If the work creates revenue and proves valuable, there is room for the responsibility and hours to grow.



Compensation



Initial hours: approximately 8–10 hours/month

Base rate: US$8–$10/hour depending on experience

Commission: 5% of qualifying revenue directly attributable to your sourcing



Commission is paid on cash actually collected, not simply contracts signed.



For example, attribution may apply where:

you identify and source a new prospect who later becomes a client;

you secure a podcast, partnership or speaking opportunity through which a new client clearly enters the business;

you originate another commercial opportunity that directly results in a new qualifying client.



Commission does not apply to:

existing clients;

previous clients;

prospects already in active conversation with us before you sourced them;

existing pipeline or pre-pipeline relationships;

opportunities you had no meaningful role in creating.



Clear attribution will be recorded so there is no ambiguity.



HOW TO APPLY



Please do not send me a generic VA cover letter.



Applications that clearly look copied-and-pasted will not be considered.



Your application should answer all seven questions below.



1. Why does this particular role appeal to you?



Maximum 150 words.



Do not tell me that you are hardworking, passionate, organised or a fast learner.



Tell me why this work appeals to you.



2. Tell me about one role where you were responsible for business development, prospecting, outreach or lead generation.



Please include:

what you were selling or promoting;

who you were trying to reach;

what your personal responsibilities were;

how you found opportunities;

what happened as a result.



3. Show me evidence of your prospect research ability.

Give me one real example of a prospect, partner or opportunity you personally identified for a previous employer/client.

You may anonymise confidential information.



Explain:



why you chose that person/opportunity;

what research you did;

why you believed it was high quality;

what happened next.



Screenshots, redacted work samples or links are welcome.



4. Tell me about your experience finding podcasts, speaking opportunities, events, communities or partnerships.



I want specific examples.



If you have sourced podcast appearances, event opportunities, strategic partnerships, guest interviews or similar opportunities, tell me:

how you found them;

how you assessed fit;

whether your client/employer was accepted;

what happened afterwards.



5. Tell me about your commission experience.



Please tell me:

what role you held;

what part of your income depended on commission/performance;

what you personally had to do to earn it;

what you liked or disliked about commission-based work.



Do not send proof at this stage.



If shortlisted, you will be asked for redacted evidence showing commission/performance earnings across three consecutive months.



6. Imagine I give you 20 LinkedIn profiles and tell you only 5 deserve further attention.



What would you look at to decide which five are strongest?



Maximum 200 words.



There is no software trick I am testing here.



I want to understand how you think.



7. Please finish your application with this exact sentence:



Right woman. Right room. Right conversation.



If that sentence is missing, I will know that you did not read the complete advertisement.



What happens next



I will review applications personally.



A small number of candidates will be invited to interview.



Shortlisted candidates may then be asked to complete a small paid practical research exercise so I can see how you think and work in a real scenario.



Final-stage candidates will be asked to provide the redacted three-month commission evidence described above.



I am not looking for the largest CV.



I am looking for someone sharp, commercially curious, trustworthy and very good at recognising opportunity.



If that sounds like you, I would love to hear from you.



Elle Nagy

Founder, Elle Nagy Limited
